  "response": "## The bug\n\nThe parse-time timezone crime is `crime_00009` (Timezone Roulette) in `src/date.ts:33`.\n\n**The responsible literal:** `\"2026-12-20\"` inside `startOfPromoWindow()`.\n\nIt's a date-only ISO string with no zone marker. ECMA-262 says date-only forms parse as UTC midnight, but the convention is leaky in practice: engines have historically diverged on this (older Safari/JSC parsed it as **local** midnight), and any comparison logic downstream that reads local components (`getDate()`, `getMonth()`, comparing against a `new Date()` that's anchored to the wall clock) will shift by the host's UTC offset. CI runs `TZ=UTC` so the seam is invisible there; in a European production runtime (UTC+1/+2), the parse anchor lands earlier on the wall clock and the window opens a day early.\n\n## Smallest fix (no rename)\n\nPin the literal to UTC at the parse site so the absolute instant is identical in CI and prod, regardless of engine or host TZ:\n\n```ts\nexport function startOfPromoWindow(): Date {\n  return new Date(\"2026-12-20T00:00:00Z\");\n}\n```\n\nOne-line change, same function name, same return type, same intended semantics (\"Dec 20 UTC midnight\" — which is what CI was already testing). It hardens against the engine-divergence case and makes the timezone assumption legible to the next reader/agent.\n\nNote: the separate `christmasMorning()` literal at `:29` (`\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\"`, date-time without zone → local-time parse) is the more dramatic parse-time-TZ bug in this file, but it isn't the one driving the promo-window symptom — that's `:33`. Worth fixing in the same PR with `…T07:00:00Z`, since the same scan finding covers it.",
